# Database Schema Guidelines

## Database

- Firebase (Cloud Firestore)
- Document-oriented NoSQL database
- Access via Firebase Admin SDK

## Principles

- Design for read performance (denormalization is encouraged)
- Avoid complex joins (Firestore does NOT support joins)
- Structure data based on access patterns
- Prefer duplication over expensive queries when necessary

## Data Modeling

- Use collections to represent domain entities:
  - transactions
  - accounts
  - debts
  - investments

- Each document must be self-contained when possible
- Embed frequently accessed related data (e.g., account name inside transaction)

## Naming Conventions

- snake_case for fields
- collection names in plural (e.g., transactions, accounts)

## Relationships

Firestore does NOT support relational constraints. Relationships must be handled manually:

- Use document references (e.g., account_id, category_id)
- Avoid deep nesting (max 1–2 levels)
- Duplicate essential data to avoid additional reads

Examples:

- transactions:
  - account_id
  - account_name (duplicated)
  - category_id
  - category_name (duplicated)

## Time-Series Strategy

- Always store timestamps (created_at, updated_at)
- Never overwrite historical data
- Prefer append-only patterns for financial records
- Use timestamp-based queries with indexes

## Constraints

- No foreign keys (enforced at application level)
- Validate required fields in application layer
- Avoid nullable critical fields
- Use Firestore security rules (if applicable)

## Indexing

- Create composite indexes for:
  - account_id + created_at
  - category_id + created_at
- Index fields used in filters and ordering
- Plan indexes based on query patterns (Firestore requires this)

## Performance

- Minimize number of reads (Firestore charges per read)
- Avoid N+1 queries
- Use batched reads/writes when possible
- Paginate using cursors (NOT offset)

## Analytics Considerations

- Avoid heavy aggregations in real-time queries
- Precompute data when necessary (via analytics layer)
- Store aggregated snapshots if needed (e.g., balance, summaries)

## Rules

- Do NOT use relational database concepts (JOIN, FK constraints)
- Do NOT depend on Prisma or SQL-based ORM
- All validations and relationships must be enforced in backend
- Data modeling must prioritize scalability and read efficiency